      "solution": "#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nusing namespace std;\ntypedef long long ll;\ntypedef vector<ll> vl;\ntypedef string str;\ntypedef vector<str> vs;\n#define foreach(a, v) for(auto& a : v)\n#define stop return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n#define vcin(v) for(auto& element : v) cin >> (element);\n/**\n * Auto-generated code below aims at helping you parse\n * the standard input according to the problem statement.\n **/\nbool c(str a, str b) {\n    return a + b > b + a;\n}\nint main()\n{\n    std::ios::sync_with_stdio(EXIT_SUCCESS); std::cin.tie(EXIT_SUCCESS); std::cout.tie(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n    ll n; cin >> n;\n    vs v(n); vcin(v);\n    sort(v.begin(), v.end(), c);\n\n    // Write an answer using cout. DON'T FORGET THE \"<< endl\"\n    // To debug: cerr << \"Debug messages...\" << endl;\n\n    foreach(x, v) cout << x;\n    cout << '\\n';\n    stop;\n}",
      "statement": "Sam is playing with [[n]] cards that has a positive integer [[A_i]] ({{1}} ≤ [[i]] ≤ [[n]]) assigned on it.\n\nHe can create a new number by appending those integers one after another. Sam wants to know the largest number that he can create. Since Sam doesn't know how to solve this problem, he asks you for help.\n\nExample Explanation:\nGiven {{5}} cards, {{123}} {{124}} {{56}} {{90}} {{9}}.\n\nAfter a bit of shuffling, you will find that rearraging the cards as {{9}} {{90}} {{56}} {{124}} {{123}} then appending all of them to attain {{99056124123}} is the largest possible integer.",
